  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2024-06-23T13:47:09.404Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "I know this is a big one but...\n\nDue to countless issues that constantly arise, i can only think that the best option for everyone would be to make Mobile (Quest incl.) and PC VRChat completely isolated.\n\nDoing so would mitigate countless social issues, drastically minimize age related issues, and cut out the painful system limitations made in the client and networking performance which were made to accommodate quest compatibility.\n\nA vast majority of young users are mobile, and they keep falling into spaces that they probably shouldn't be getting into.  The most wide-sweeping solution would be to cut mobile users into a more strictly moderated space, and let the pc space remain more or less as it is, while continuing work on content gating to serve user preference for self-censorship.\n\nI don't see the idea of age verification, even if by third party sources, being a particularly strong or healthy solution overall, especially in this day and age where pricelessly valuable anonymity is becoming an endangered species.  Keeping the universal standard of honour system birth year inputting or just saying \"yes i'm 18\" puts the onus and responsibility on the end user and/or guardians rather than the platform, and little more should be necessary.\n\nWith this change VRChat could also split it's marketing to be more honest about what the more adult platform is about, while still marketing the mobile version to younger audiences.  Allowing the benefits of both demographics to continue to bolster VRChat's market value while significantly reducing unavoidable issues caused by their crossover.",
